From 99ba8583c5cbf1720ee9fc1efce351aebecfab1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 22:07:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/13] nvk/rm: fixes from the first run on an RTX 5090 (GB202,
 GSP)

vkCreateDevice now succeeds on RM 610.57.04 under Conduit. What the
first run found:

- NV01_MEMORY_SYSTEM_OS_DESCRIPTOR through NV_ESC_RM_ALLOC fails with
  NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED: RM takes a user address only through
  NV_ESC_RM_ALLOC_MEMORY, where the Unix escape layer pins the pages.
  Use librmclient's new crm_alloc_os_descriptor when it is there.
- RM aligns a default-page-size NV50_MEMORY_VIRTUAL to 64 KiB, and to
  2 MiB (offset and size) once it is 2 MiB or larger, even with
  FIXED_ADDRESS_ALLOCATE.  Align our ranges the same way; log when RM
  moves one anyway.
- The timeline vk_sync type advertised GPU_MULTI_WAIT, a binary-only
  feature (vk_sync_type_validate asserts).
- A new FERMI_VASPACE_A cannot hold a GR context under GSP:
  VA_INTERNAL_LIMIT pins RM's internal range to [4 GiB, 4.5 GiB), which
  a GSP client reserves entirely for GSP-RM, so the 3D object fails with
  NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY mapping its context buffers.  Use the device's VA
  space (FERMI_VASPACE_A index GPU_DEVICE) like NVIDIA's driver;
  RESTRICT_RESERVED_VALIMITS on the device is refused by GSP.
- The subcontext must be SYNC (VEID 0): GSP-RM refuses the 3D object on
  an ASYNC subcontext with NV_ERR_INVALID_OBJECT.  Also bind the channel
  to GR (NVA06F_CTRL_CMD_BIND) before allocating engine objects, as
  NVIDIA's driver does.
- cls_m2mf came out 0 on Blackwell (0xcd40 was outside the range), which
  NVK takes for Fermi and pushes on an M2MF subchannel it has not got.

